== prefix
# The relations a fact can be added to or removed from:
# imports(from, to)  A package importing another.
# owner(pkg, person)  Who owns a package.
# pkg(name, license: one of "MIT", "Apache-2.0", "BSD-3-Clause")  A package and its license.

# Write the facts the text states, one per line, using only the relations above:
#   + relation("value", ...) from "the words of the text that state it"
# for a fact the text states, and
#   - relation("value", ...) from "the words of the text that say so"
# for one it says no longer holds. Values are "quoted" text or numbers. After from, quote the
# text's own words exactly as they appear. Reply with the lines alone, or nothing if the text
# states no fact.

# What the data is about:
# Go packages, what they import, their licenses and owners.

# Examples:
# util is BSD licensed (BSD-3-Clause) and imports nothing.
+ pkg("util", "BSD-3-Clause") from "util is BSD licensed (BSD-3-Clause)"

== license
text: log imports util, and log is released under the Apache 2.0 license.
tries: [type ]
feedback 1:
line 2: query: pkg's "license" argument cannot be "Apache 2.0", did you mean "Apache-2.0"? (it holds one of: MIT, Apache-2.0, BSD-3-Clause)
  + pkg("log", "Apache 2.0") from "released under the Apache 2.0 license"
               ^
fix: did you mean "Apache-2.0"?

Write all the lines again, fixed.
changes:
+ imports("log", "util") [text:0-16]
+ pkg("log", "Apache-2.0") [text:29-66]
== owner
text: Bob owns app now; alice handed it over last week.
tries: []
changes:
- owner("app", "alice")
+ owner("app", "bob") [text:0-16]
== removed
text: As of this release app no longer imports api.
tries: []
changes:
- imports("app", "api")
